Yes, creating a dedicated API to fetch the Roles from the database is the absolute best enterprise practice. This way, if you add a new role or change a name in the DB, the frontend automatically adapts without any code changes!
We will do this in two quick phases: **Create the Java API** and **Consume it in React**.
### PHASE 1: Create the Java API
**1. Add to your Interface (VoucherRequestService.java)**
Open your interface file and add this method signature:
```java
Map<String, String> getActiveRoles();

```
**2. Add to your Service Implementation (VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java)**
Open your implementation file and paste this method anywhere inside the class. This will fetch all active roles and map the ID to the Name.
```java
    @Override
    public Map<String, String> getActiveRoles() {
        String sql = "SELECT ROLE_ID, ROLE_NAME FROM ROLES WHERE ROLE_STATUS = 'ACTIVE'";
        
        // Use ResultSetExtractor to build a single Map of { "9001": "TCSGLIF" }
        return jdbcTemplate.query(sql, rs -> {
            Map<String, String> roleMap = new HashMap<>();
            while (rs.next()) {
                roleMap.put(String.valueOf(rs.getLong("ROLE_ID")), rs.getString("ROLE_NAME"));
            }
            return roleMap;
        });
    }

```
**3. Add to your Controller (VoucherPostingController.java)**
Open your controller and add this endpoint so the frontend can call it:
```java
    @GetMapping("/active-roles")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>> getActiveRoles() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(voucherRequestService.getActiveRoles());
    }

```
*Restart your Spring Boot application after making these changes.*
### PHASE 2: Update React to use the API
Now we will update JournalAuthorization.jsx to fetch this data when the page loads, store it in state, and use it in the stepper.
**1. Add a State Variable for the Roles**
Find your useState declarations (around line 155) and add this:
```javascript
  const [dbRoleMap, setDbRoleMap] = useState({}); // Stores the dynamic roles from DB

```
**2. Fetch the Roles on Page Load**
Add a new useEffect right below your existing fetchPendingBatches logic to call the new API:
```javascript
  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchRoles = async () => {
      try {
        // Fetch the { "9001": "TCSGLIF" } map from our new API
        const rolesData = await callApi("/JS/voucher/active-roles", null, "GET");
        if (rolesData && isMounted.current) {
          setDbRoleMap(rolesData);
        }
      } catch (err) {
        console.error("Failed to fetch roles mapping", err);
      }
    };
    fetchRoles();
  }, [callApi]);

```
**3. Replace the Hardcoded Function**
Find your getRoleDisplayName function near the top of your component and replace it entirely with this dynamic version:
```javascript
  const getRoleDisplayName = (roleValue) => {
    if (!roleValue || roleValue === "null" || roleValue === "System") return "System Execution";
    
    // Now it looks inside the dynamic DB map instead of a hardcoded one!
    return dbRoleMap[String(roleValue)] || roleValue; 
  };

```
### How this works perfectly:
 1. When the page loads, React calls /api/voucher/active-roles.
 2. The Java backend runs SELECT ROLE_ID, ROLE_NAME FROM ROLES.
 3. Java returns a JSON object like: {"9001": "TCSGLIF", "9002": "SBIGLIF"}.
 4. React saves that object into dbRoleMap.
 5. When the Stepper draws the circles, it passes 9001 into getRoleDisplayName(), which looks at your dynamic map and perfectly outputs **"TCSGLIF"**.
No more hardcoded roles!
